From: MahanHD <mahanheidari2003@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: clear carveout buffers when they are allocated

When the display has no IOMMU, msm_use_mmu() is false and every GEM
buffer, the GPU's included, comes from the VRAM carveout. The carveout
is cleared once when it is allocated, but get_pages_vram() hands out
pages as their previous owner left them, while shmem buffers always
start zeroed. On the Xperia Z1 Compact (a330, display on the carveout)
that shows as "Division by zero" from retire_submits(): a3xx never
writes the per-submit stats in the ring's memptrs, so retire_submit()
reads leftovers, and do_div() divides by the low half of a garbage
elapsed time. The count changed from boot to boot. User buffers
started with stale data in the same way.

Clear carveout pages in get_pages_vram(), through the kernel's cached
alias, and write the zeroes back before the GPU or display can see the
buffer: write-combined buffers already get sync_for_device(); cached
carveout buffers now get it too, followed by sync_for_cpu() so that no
cache line is written back over the zeroes later.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c	2026-10-11 19:57:55.831255851 +0400
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c	2026-10-11 19:57:55.847914114 +0400
@@ -165,6 +165,14 @@
 		paddr += PAGE_SIZE;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Unlike shmem pages, carveout pages come back exactly as their last
+	 * owner left them. Clear them here, through the kernel's cached alias;
+	 * get_pages() then writes the zeroes back to memory.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < npages; i++)
+		clear_highpage(p[i]);
+
 	return p;
 }
 
@@ -206,8 +214,17 @@
 		/* For non-cached buffers, ensure the new pages are clean
 		 * because display controller, GPU, etc. are not coherent:
 		 */
-		if (msm_obj->flags & MSM_BO_WC)
+		if (msm_obj->flags & MSM_BO_WC) {
+			sync_for_device(msm_obj);
+		} else if (!use_pages(obj)) {
+			/*
+			 * A cached carveout buffer: get the zeroes written
+			 * by get_pages_vram() to memory too, and drop the
+			 * cache lines so none is written back over them.
+			 */
 			sync_for_device(msm_obj);
+			sync_for_cpu(msm_obj);
+		}
 
 		update_lru(obj);
 	}
